<article class="env-lemma" id="0H2X">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H2X">Lemma <span data-tag="0H2X">66.4.11</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$. Let $p : \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \to X$ and $q : \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(L) \to X$ be morphisms where $K$ and $L$ are fields. Assume $p$ and $q$ determine the same point of $|X|$ and $p$ is a monomorphism. Then $q$ factors uniquely through $p$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $p$ and $q$ define the same point of $|X|$, we see that the scheme 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  Y = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(K) \times _{p, X, q} \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(L)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is nonempty. Since the base change of a monomorphism is a monomorphism this means that the projection morphism $Y \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(L)$ is a monomorphism. Hence $Y = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(L)$, see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/03DP" data-tag="03DP">26.23.11</a>. We conclude that $q$ factors through $p$. Uniqueness comes from the fact that $p$ is a monomorphism. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>